  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Goldschmidt's power (2 homers, 2 RBI) kept them ahead, but Detroit's run in the first tied it until a sixth-inning go-ahead moment broke the deadlock and held through nine.",
    "what_this_means": "The win stretched a two-game streak, and the formula stayed intact: pitching held, relief bridged cleanly into the ninth with Bednar sealing it. Weathers delivered six innings of one-run baseball on 6 hits. That's the kind of start that wins ballgames when the lineup has any patience at all. Goldschmidt's pair (both solo shots) mattered because they arrived in a game where scoring was hard. The bullpen trio (Doval, Cruz, Bednar combining 2.3 clean innings with four strikeouts) did not hand the game back. This is what a controlled game looks like: not dominant, not loose, but anchored. The offense left two on in scoring position and never recovered those at-bats, yet the lead held because nobody gave it away late. Two runs in the sixth turned it. That's the margin they're running on now. Three straight one-run wins. That math gets harder.",
    "what_to_watch": "Boston arrives tonight with Connelly Early (3.64 ERA) facing Cam Schlittler, who's posted a 2.42 ERA over his last four starts. The division doesn't bend for two-win streaks.",
